\section{Related Work}
The use of LLMs for synthetic data generation has grown rapidly. Initial approaches involved simple zero-shot\cite{ref_zs} or few-shot prompting\cite{ref_fs}. More recent methods include attributed prompting \cite{ref_attr} and multi-step generation\cite{ref_steps} for better control and diversity.
Concurrently, techniques have emerged for automatically optimizing prompts to improve LLM performance on various tasks\cite{ref_ape4synth}. Among these, PACE\cite{ref_pace} stands out for its iterative, feedback-driven optimization, making it a suitable candidate for improving prompt quality without relying on real data. While such methods have not been originally applied to synthetic data generation, the core principle of automated prompt optimization is highly relevant for improving data quality in this context. Our work builds on this idea and, to the best of our knowledge, is the first to evaluate a technique like PACE specifically for generating synthetic data.

In RE, synthetic data research has traditionally focused on data augmentation techniques like back-translation, paraphrasing, or rule-based substitutions\cite{b6,b7}. Cheng et al.\cite{b8} explored LLMs (GPT-J) for augmentation. However, augmentation methods depend on existing data and offer limited novelty and control. Our approach focuses on generating entirely new datasets from scratch using LLMs, guided by a PL approach for systematic control. This iteration builds on our initial work\cite{ref_synthlinev0} by exploring generation methods such as multi-sample prompting and PACE, along with a similarity-based curation approach, across a wider set of RE classification tasks, providing a more comprehensive evaluation than previously available in the RE literature.
