\section{The Synthline v1 Design}
This research follows the DSR methodology\cite{ref_dsr}, with iterative design and evaluation of artifacts. This paper presents the second iteration of Synthline, building on insights from the initial version\cite{ref_synthlinev0}. We retain the PL-based control approach, extending it with new generation strategies and a curation approach.

Synthline employs a systematic configuration-based approach where synthetic requirements are generated by LLMs through prompts whose parameters can be entirely controlled by the user. These configuration parameters are formally specified using an FM in which each variable configuration parameter (such as domain, language, or stakeholder type) is defined as a feature, with all features organized hierarchically and linked by dependencies and constraints that ensure semantically correct parameter value combinations. Synthline v1 offers 18 parameters for controlling synthetic requirements generation; these parameters are formalized in a tree-structured model consisting of 4 main feature groups organized across 3 hierarchical levels with cross-cutting dependencies and alternative group constraints, enabling the systematic generation of multiple parameter combinations through a PL engineering approach.
%
\subsection{Synthline v1 Architecture}
%
Synthline v1 extends the original PL-based architecture while preserving its use of an FM as the foundation for controlled and systematic data generation. As illustrated in Figure \ref{synthlinev1}, the enhanced architecture keeps the end-to-end workflow intact—from the Web UI Configurator to the generation of synthetic data—while introducing a conditional optimization branch that enables the integration of the PACE method detailed in \ref{subsec:pace}.
%
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.55]{synthlinev1.png}
\caption{High-level architecture of the enhanced Synthline (v1).} \label{synthlinev1}
\end{figure}
%
The workflow begins with the Web UI Configurator processing user-selected features and generating an FM-based configuration that flows to the Promptline component.
Promptline interprets the FM-based configuration to generate a series of atomic configurations. When users select multiple values for certain features, Synthline systematically creates atomic configurations—individual combinations where each feature has exactly one specific value. This decomposition ensures complete coverage of all possible combinations defined by the user's multi-selections, with each atomic configuration serving as a distinct, fully-specified context for prompt generation. For each atomic configuration, Promptline constructs a corresponding prompt using standardized templates that support both single-sample and multi-sample generation strategies, based on the \texttt{samples\_per\_prompt} feature, which defines whether each prompt requests one requirement or several (see \ref{subsec:fmevolution}).

If selected by the user, Promptline also orchestrates an automated prompt optimization process by invoking the PACE module. When the default prompting strategy is used, prompts flow directly from Promptline to the Generator. However, when PACE optimization is enabled, Promptline coordinates with the PACE component to optimize the prompts before they proceed to generation. The primary role of Promptline is thus to systematically prepare and manage the prompts that will drive the LLM.

The PACE component operates through bidirectional communication with the LLM Service, performing iterative optimization using the Actor-Critic framework detailed in \ref{subsec:pace}. This process refines the initial prompts generated by Promptline, handling multiple atomic configurations in parallel to maintain systematic coverage of the feature space, while operating asynchronously to enable efficient processing during potentially lengthy optimization cycles.

Once the prompts are finalized—either directly from Promptline or after PACE optimization—the Generator module orchestrates the main data generation phase. It manages large-scale interaction with the LLM Service, using the prepared prompts and operational generation parameters from the user’s configuration to produce the synthetic requirements.

Interaction with various LLMs is handled by the LLM Service component, which acts as a unified client that abstracts the specific communication protocols and API details of different LLM providers.

Finally, the Output Handler is responsible for persisting the generated synthetic data. It takes the structured samples from the Generator and saves them to the file system in the user-specified format.

Compared to Synthline v0, v1 introduces key advancements: (1) the PACE module for automated prompt optimization; (2) multi-sample generation capability, which required enhanced prompt templates and parsing mechanisms to reliably handle structured LLM outputs while maintaining compatibility with single-sample generation; and (3) an enhanced web-based interface that provides real-time feedback and progress tracking during generation and optimization.

The modular architecture ensures that each enhancement can be independently validated and configured, supporting the experimental evaluation posed by our research questions while preserving the controlled variability that distinguishes Synthline from ad-hoc generation methods.
%
\subsection{Feature Model Evolution}
\label{subsec:fmevolution}
The FM retains the four core features established in Synthline v0—\texttt{Generator}, \texttt{Artifact}, \texttt{MLTask}, and \texttt{Output}—which provide systematic control over LLM configuration, generated data characteristics, classification task specification, and output formatting respectively. The \texttt{MLTask} feature continues to focus on the \texttt{Classification} subfeature with its mandatory \texttt{Label} and \texttt{LabelDescription} components that define target classification categories and their definitions. The \texttt{Output} feature maintains its \texttt{OutputFormat} selection between \texttt{CSV} and \texttt{JSON} formats, while the \texttt{SubsetSize} parameter continues to enable control over the volume of data generated in each run. The \texttt{Artifact} feature continues to enable fine-grained control over requirement generation through its \texttt{SpecificationLevel}, \texttt{RequirementSource}, \texttt{SpecificationFormat}, \texttt{Domain}, and \texttt{Language} subfeatures, allowing users to specify diverse combinations that result in multiple atomic configurations covering the desired feature space.

The underlying FM has been updated in this iteration to support the experimental variables investigated in our research questions. Key additions include enhancements within the \texttt{Generator} feature group: \texttt{samples\_per\_prompt}, an integer input specifying how many requirements to request in a single prompt (e.g., 1 or 20); \texttt{prompt\_approach}, a selection between \texttt{Default} (standard template-based prompting) and \texttt{PACE} (automated prompt optimization); and PACE-specific parameters (\texttt{pace\_iterations}, \texttt{pace\_actors}, \texttt{pace\_candidates}) that become available when \texttt{prompt\_approach} is set to \texttt{PACE}, allowing users to configure the optimization process.
%
\subsection{PACE}
\label{subsec:pace}
%
To explore advanced prompt engineering techniques for enhancing synthetic data quality, we integrated PACE\cite{ref_pace} into Synthline. PACE is an iterative approach designed to automatically optimize prompts for specific LLM tasks.

As illustrated in Figure \ref{pace}, the core PACE loop involves using an LLM in two key roles. An \textbf{LLM Actor} executes the current prompt to generate synthetic requirements, while an \textbf{LLM Critic} evaluates these generated requirements against the prompt's objectives and provides textual critiques suggesting improvements. These critiques are then used to automatically update and refine the prompt for the next iteration (Update). This cycle repeats for a predefined number of iterations, aiming to find a prompt that maximizes a specific scoring function based on the quality of the generated outputs.
%
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{PACE.png}
\caption{The PACE Approach.} \label{pace}
\end{figure}
%

In our implementation within Synthline, we employed PACE specifically to optimize prompts for generating diverse sets of requirements. Therefore, we configured the scoring function within PACE to maximize the average cosine distance between the embeddings of the generated requirements within a batch, encouraging semantic diversity. %(see Table \ref{tab:pace_params}).
