\subsubsection{Functional vs. Non-Functional Classification}
This binary classification task distinguishes between functional and non-functional requirements. We use the Functional-Quality dataset from Dalpiaz et al.\cite{ref_dalpiaz}, which contains 956 requirements from multiple open-source and closed-source projects including reclassifications of PROMISE, Dronology, Wasp, Leeds, and ReqView. According to the original paper\cite{ref_dalpiaz}, a requirement is classified as \textit{Functional} when it possesses functional aspects (functional goals or functional constraints). The dataset contains 587 functional requirements and 387 non-functional requirements.

%
\subsubsection{Quality vs. Non-Quality Classification}
This task focuses on identifying quality requirements. Using the same Functional-Quality dataset, a requirement is classified as \textit{Quality} when it possesses quality aspects (quality goals or quality constraints)\cite{ref_dalpiaz}. The dataset distribution for this task includes 522 quality requirements and 434 non-quality requirements.

%
\subsubsection{Security vs. Non-Security Classification}
This binary classification task identifies security-related requirements. We use the SecReq dataset created by Knauss et al.\cite{ref_knauss}, containing 510 requirements collected from three security-focused projects: Common Electronic Purse (ePurse), Customer Premises Network (CPN), and Global Platform Spec (GPS). According to the original paper\cite{ref_knauss}, a requirement is classified as \textit{Security} when it contributes to a security objective and aligns with Common Criteria functional components (especially at the element level). The dataset contains 187 security-related requirements and 323 non-security related requirements.
%
