\subsection{Synthetic Data Generation}
%
We configured Synthline to generate datasets for each of the four tasks. As the backend LLM generator, we used the recent flagship OpenAI model, \textbf{GPT-4.1 nano}, selected for its optimal trade-off between performance, speed, and cost as of our experimentation period in late April 2025 \footnote{OpenAI, Introducing GPT-4.1, \url{https://openai.com/index/gpt-4-1/}}. The base configuration used for generation is outlined in Table \ref{tab:gen_config}.
Following \cite{b34}, Temperature of 1.0 and TopP of 1.0 were chosen, as these settings have been empirically shown to produce high-quality, diverse synthetic data while maintaining semantic coherence.
Both High-Level and Detailed specification levels were included to capture requirements at different granularities, reflecting the range of abstraction levels commonly found in real requirements documentation.
All four Requirement Sources (End Users, Business Managers, Development Team, Regulatory Bodies) were included to ensure comprehensive stakeholder perspective coverage, as different sources typically produce requirements with distinct linguistic patterns and priorities.
For Specification Format, we included Natural Language (NL), Constrained NL, and User Story formats. We excluded Use Case format because its fundamentally different structure creates a significant mismatch with our concise, single-statement baseline requirements and makes reliable parsing difficult. The selected formats align better with the textual characteristics of our baseline requirements and provide sufficient diversity in structure and formality.
English was selected to align with all real baseline datasets used for evaluation, ensuring fair comparison and avoiding potential cross-linguistic performance artifacts that could confound our utility assessments.
We selected three domains—Telecommunications, Healthcare, and Enterprise Data Management—to increase variability and semantic diversity while maintaining a manageable number of atomic configurations. These domains represent distinct technical vocabularies and requirement patterns, enhancing the robustness of our synthetic data without creating excessive experimental complexity.
The CSV output format was chosen for its simplicity and direct compatibility with the real datasets' format, facilitating seamless integration with our evaluation pipeline.
Notably, we excluded the RequirementTypes subfeature (e.g., Functional, Performance, Interface) primarily to reduce the combinatorial complexity of atomic configurations, making the experimental design more manageable.
