\subsection{Synthetic Data Curation}
%
To investigate RQ3, we applied a curation pipeline to the generated datasets (synthetic\_1, synthetic\_20, synthetic\_20\_pace) to create curated\_synthetic\_* variants. This pipeline involves three steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Deduplication:} Removal of exact duplicate requirements.
    \item \textbf{Similarity Filtering:} Removal of the top 20\% most semantically similar samples within the dataset. Similarity is calculated using cosine similarity on Sentence-BERT (all-mpnet-base-v2) embeddings. The average similarity of each sample to all others determines the ranking for removal. We chose to remove the top 20\% most semantically similar samples, an admittedly somewhat arbitrary threshold selected to provide a moderate level of curation that would be sufficient to observe measurable effects on diversity metrics while preserving the majority of generated data. This threshold represents a pragmatic choice for our experimental investigation rather than an empirically optimized value. The 20\% removal rate ensures sufficient data retention for reliable classifier training while creating a meaningful contrast with the uncurated datasets for comparison purposes.
    \item \textbf{Class Balancing:} Undersampling the majority class(es) within each dataset to match the number of samples in the minority class, ensuring perfect class balance.
\end{enumerate}
This curation process enhances diversity by reducing redundancy and balancing each dataset to avoid bias toward majority classes.
%
%
\subsection{Evaluation Setup}
%
We evaluated the generated datasets along two dimensions: diversity and utility.
%
\subsubsection{Diversity Evaluation}
We calculated diversity metrics for all dataset variants (real, synthetic, curated) for each classification task. The metrics include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{INGF (Inter-sample N-gram Frequency):} This metric assesses lexical and phrase-level diversity by measuring how frequently the same n-grams (contiguous sequences of n words, here typically trigrams) appear across different samples in the dataset. Formally, we compute the average frequency of unique n-grams across all text samples. A high INGF score means the dataset contains repeated phrases across multiple samples, suggesting lower diversity at the expression level. Conversely, a lower INGF score reflects greater lexical variety and less phrase repetition between samples.
    \item \textbf{APS (Average Pairwise Similarity):} This metric captures semantic diversity by computing the average cosine similarity between Sentence-BERT embeddings of text pairs. For each dataset, we compute embeddings for all samples, then calculate cosine similarities between all possible pairs of samples. The APS score is the mean of these pairwise similarities. A higher APS indicates that samples tend to convey semantically similar content, while a lower APS suggests richer semantic diversity — the samples are more different in meaning.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Utility Evaluation}
To evaluate the utility of the synthetic data, we adopted the standard train-on-synthetic, test-on-real paradigm. This approach measures how well models trained purely on synthetic data generalize to unseen, real-world examples, providing a direct assessment of the synthetic data's practical value for downstream tasks.
Specifically, each synthetic dataset variant (e.g., synthetic\_1, curated\_synthetic\_20\_pace), classifiers were trained exclusively using that variant. These models, along with a baseline model trained solely on 70\% of the available real data, were then consistently evaluated on a held-out test set comprising the remaining 30\% of the real data specific to each classification task. This protocol allows for direct comparison of the utility provided by different synthetic data generation strategies relative to each other and the real-data baseline.
We used BERT-base-uncased\cite{ref_bert} as the downstream model for all experiments.
To obtain statistically reliable performance estimates and mitigate the effects of stochasticity inherent in model training, we conducted 5 independent runs for each dataset configuration, varying the random seed for each run.
For each configuration, hyperparameter optimization was performed initially using Optuna\cite{ref_optuna} (32 trials) on a 90\%/10\% stratified train/validation split derived from the first run's data. This process identified the optimal learning rate, batch size, weight decay, and number of training epochs by maximizing the weighted F1-score on the validation set. These best hyperparameters were then held constant across all 5 runs for that configuration, ensuring consistency while assessing performance variability.
Model performance was quantified using weighted Precision, Recall, and F1-score. The final reported values represent the mean and standard deviation calculated across the 5 independent runs for each configuration. Weighted metrics were selected to provide an unbiased performance measure by accounting for any potential class imbalance within the real test sets.
