\section{Validity Threats}
In our study, we identified several threats to validity across different dimensions.
\textbf{Internal validity} threats primarily come from our experimental design choices. The selection of GPT-4.1 nano as the sole LLM backend introduces potential model-specific biases, as different LLMs may show varying capabilities in synthetic data generation. Our PACE parameter configuration followed recommendations from the original paper but was not empirically optimized for synthetic requirements generation specifically, potentially limiting the effectiveness of prompt optimization. Additionally, using cosine distance as the PACE scoring function may not align well with classification utility - prompts could generate semantically diverse but low-quality requirements that still receive high optimization scores.
\textbf{External validity} is limited by several factors. Our evaluation focused exclusively on English-language requirements from software development contexts, limiting generalizability to other languages or domain-specific requirements practices. The four classification tasks, while covering important RE areas, represent only a subset of possible requirements analysis tasks. The real datasets used for evaluation are relatively small and may not reflect the complexity and variability of industrial requirements. Furthermore, our evaluation using BERT-base-uncased as the downstream classifier may not generalize to other ML architectures or more recent language models.
\textbf{Construct validity} concerns arise from our choice of diversity metrics. The INGF and APS measures, while established, may not capture all aspects of meaningful linguistic and semantic diversity relevant to requirements. Our focus on classification utility, while appropriate for our research questions, represents one specific aspect of synthetic data value in RE.
\textbf{Conclusion validity} faced threats from the relatively small number of experimental runs (5 per configuration) and the inherent stochasticity in both LLM generation and neural network training. Additionally, our similarity-based curation approach used fixed thresholds (top 20\% removal) that were not empirically validated as optimal for each task.
%
%
%
\section{Conclusion}
This paper presented Synthline v1, an enhanced PL approach for generating synthetic requirements data using LLMs. Through systematic evaluation across four requirements classification tasks, we investigated how different generation strategies and curation techniques affect synthetic data quality.
Our key findings show that \textbf{generating multiple requirements per prompt substantially improves both utility and diversity} compared to single-sample generation, with F1-score improvements of 6-44 percentage points across tasks. This approach appears to help LLMs generate more coherent and varied requirement sets.
\textbf{PACE-based prompt optimization produced mixed results} - it consistently improved phrase-level diversity but had task-dependent effects on classification performance. While PACE dramatically helped functional requirements classification (+32.5 percentage points F1-score), it hurt performance on security and defects tasks. This suggests that diversity-focused optimization doesn't universally improve classification utility.
Surprisingly, \textbf{similarity-based curation improved diversity but often hurt classification performance}. This reveals that removing similar examples may actually remove valuable training information, challenging the assumption that higher diversity automatically means better utility for ML.
Most importantly, \textbf{synthetic requirements can match or exceed human-authored requirements for certain classification tasks}. Synthetic data achieved better performance for security (+7.8 percentage points F1-score) and defects classification (+15.4 percentage points), offering a scalable solution to dataset scarcity in RE research.

\textbf{Future work} should explore better alignment between prompt optimization objectives and downstream task performance, investigate curation techniques that preserve informative examples, and extend evaluation to additional RE tasks beyond classification. Cross-linguistic studies and industrial case studies would strengthen the generalizability of these findings.
