\begin{abstract}
The rise of deepfake audio and hate speech, powered by advanced text-to-speech, threatens online safety. We present \textbf{SynHate}, the first multilingual dataset for detecting hate speech in synthetic audio, spanning 37 languages. SynHate uses a novel four-class scheme: \textit{Real-normal, Real-hate, Fake-normal, and Fake-hate}. Built from MuTox and ADIMA datasets, it captures diverse hate speech patterns globally and in India. We evaluate five leading self-supervised models (Whisper-small/medium, XLS-R, AST, mHuBERT), finding notable performance differences by language, with Whisper-small performing best overall. Cross-dataset generalization remains a challenge. By releasing SynHate and baseline code, we aim to advance robust, culturally sensitive, and multilingual solutions against synthetic hate speech. The dataset is available at \url{https://www.iab-rubric.org/resources}.
% The convergence of hate speech and deepfake audio, driven by advanced text-to-speech technologies, poses a significant threat to online safety. To address this critical challenge, we introduce \textbf{SynHate}, the first comprehensive multilingual dataset specifically designed for audio-based hate speech spoofing detection. SynHate uniquely combines hate speech detection with synthetic audio identification across 37 languages. It employs a novel four-class framework consisting of \textit{Real-normal, Real-hate, Fake-normal, and Fake-hate}. Our dataset construction leverages the MuTox and ADIMA datasets, effectively capturing diverse patterns of hate speech from global and Indian contexts. Extensive experiments using five state-of-the-art self-supervised models (Whisper-small, Whisper-medium, XLS-R, AST, and mHuBERT) demonstrate significant performance variability across languages, with Whisper-small achieving the highest overall accuracy. Additionally, our analysis highlights substantial challenges associated with cross-dataset generalization. By publicly releasing SynHate and accompanying baseline code, we aim to facilitate the development of robust, culturally aware, and multilingual methods to combat synthetic hate speech, fostering safer and more inclusive online environments. The dataset is available at \url{https://www.iab-rubric.org/resources}.

%The convergence of hate speech and deepfake audio, fueled by advanced text-to-speech technologies, has created a formidable threat to online safety. To address this pressing challenge, we introduce \textbf{SynHate}, the first comprehensive dataset for multilingual audio hate speech spoofing detection. SynHate bridges a critical gap by uniting hate speech detection with audio spoofing identification across 37 languages through a novel four-class classification framework, Real-normal, Real-hate, Fake-normal, and Fake-hate, thereby enabling researchers to confront the dual challenges of detecting both hate speech and synthetic audio. Our work details the dataset construction methodology, which integrates samples from the MuTox and ADIMA datasets to capture diverse hate speech patterns in both global and Indic contexts. We conduct extensive evaluations using five state-of-the-art self-supervised models, including Whisper-small, Whisper-medium, XLS-R, AST, and mHuBERT. Our experiments reveal substantial performance variability across languages and models, with Whisper-small achieving superior overall accuracy, and expose significant challenges in cross-dataset generalization. By providing SynHate and releasing the accompanying baseline codes publicly, we aim to catalyze the development of robust, culturally attuned, and multilingual defenses against the growing threat of synthetic hate speech, ultimately contributing to safer and more inclusive online environments.
