To further illustrate the versatility and effectiveness of the proposed method for the 5-toohead system, a 3D Benchy model was printed using a spatially complex gradient defined by Equation~\ref{eq:3d_gradient}~\cite{3DBenchy2015}. For this demonstration, a gradient density parameter of $\alpha = 25\,\mathrm{mm}$ was chosen, determining the scale and frequency of the gradient variation. The Benchy geometry was sliced using Strategy 1 and fabricated using the Prusa XL, employing a two-color gradient transitioning from yellow to blue filaments. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:benchy}, the printed model accurately reproduces the intended spatial gradient, clearly illustrating precise filament placement. Notably, our process directly converted a single input mesh and 3D gradient function to G-code, eliminating the need for intermediate boundary surfaces required by conventional methods, thereby significantly simplifying the workflow for multi-material gradient fabrication.


