    \label{fig:benchy}
\end{figure}

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    V_{\text{blue}} &= \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi x}{\alpha}\right)\cos\left(\frac{2\pi y}{\alpha}\right)\sin\left(\frac{2\pi z}{\alpha}\right), \\
    V_{\text{yellow}} &= 1 - \left[\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi x}{\alpha}\right)\cos\left(\frac{2\pi y}{\alpha}\right)\sin\left(\frac{2\pi z}{\alpha}\right)\right].
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:3d_gradient}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
Figure~\ref{fig:big_comparison} illustrates the capabilities of our proposed slicing method across multiple gradient types, systems, and process parameters. Columns one through four represent single-axis gradients along different spatial orientations, while column five highlights our novel contribution:  slicing for arbitrary 3D, multi-axis gradients and geometries. Boxes shaded in light blue indicate gradient configurations demonstrated for the first time in this work, whereas white boxes represent gradient capabilities previously established in the literature. Albeit these prior works required custom G-code for each different geometry. Additionally, this research provides the first demonstration of radial and circumferential gradients specifically tailored for temperature-responsive foaming filaments.

It is important to emphasize that all examples depicted in Figure \ref{fig:big_comparison}, including previously reported gradients, were successfully fabricated using our approach. This underscores that our proposed method not only encompasses but substantially extends beyond existing gradient-printing techniques. A key strength of our methodology lies in its complete automation and generalized applicability: a single OpenVCAD design can be directly sliced into ready-to-print G-code for diverse printing systems and gradient types, without the need for manual design specific G-code generation or intermediate boundary-surface processing.


\section{Conclusion}
This paper presents a novel method for generating toolpaths in functionally graded additive manufacturing, enabling smooth and continuous transitions in material composition. Unlike previous works that focused on single-axis gradients, our approach leverages implicit design representations and iso-contouring to support complex, multi-dimensional gradients. By embedding the material distribution directly into the design stage, our slicing methods translate high-level gradient intentions into executable G-code automatically.

We introduce two complementary strategies for toolpath planning. The first preserves traditional slicing structures and integrates local composition control within conventional wall, infill, and skin operations. By segmenting these standard toolpaths into gradient-informed regions and incorporating intelligent path ordering, this approach effectively manages non-instantaneous transitions. It also allows automate placement ``zippers'' at material interfaces to improve adhesion between dissimilar materials. The second strategy embraces the implicit gradient fields fully, employing a direct ``printing against the gradient'' approach. This results in highly resolved gradations without the need for purge towers, though at the cost of not employing conventional print structures. Notably, our method can be leveraged to functionally grade any process parameter that does not change its state instantaneously. We demonstrate our method for mixing systems, fixed-number multi-toolhead systems, and with temperature responsive foaming materials. This opens the door to continuous variation in density, stiffness, or other mechanical and functional properties—all without sharp interfaces.

The utility of these methods transcends single-axis or single-parameter gradients. Complex 2D and 3D material distribution fields become accessible, offering improved design freedom and enabling designers to target custom properties at specific locations in a part. 

Future work could explore several avenues to further enhance the flexibility and capabilities of this approach. First, extending the method to three or more input materials will allow for a richer palette of color, mechanical, thermal, or conductive properties. Combining multiple distinct materials with different fundamental properties—such as elastomers or conductive filaments will enable an even broader range of applications. Additionally, applying these strategies to active-mixing hotends rather than the passive system used in this study may reduce dead volume and further improve gradient fidelity. Another promising direction involves demonstrating the method for direct ink write (DIW) systems, where microfluidic mixing can produce intricate gradients in a wide range of inks, from ceramics to biological materials. Similarly, application of this work to support mixing Directed Energy Deposition systems offers another direction of potential investigation.

To support continued exploration and advancement in the field of multi-material additive manufacturing, we are releasing the presented slicing methods as an open-source extension to OpenVCAD. This contribution aims to accelerate research into functionally graded materials, mixing extruder systems, and foaming filaments. By providing a robust slicing method capable of handling complex gradients and continuous material transitions, our work directly addresses a significant barrier previously limiting application-driven research in multi-material additive manufacturing.

