% 1. ADS is not perfect
Autonomous Driving Systems (ADSs)
% have rapidly evolved to enhance transportation, yet they
continue to face safety-critical risks due to the inherent limitations in their design and performance capabilities.
% 2. Repair should be used
Online repair plays a crucial role in mitigating such limitations, ensuring the runtime safety and reliability of ADSs.
% 3. Existing solutions
Existing online repair solutions enforce ADS compliance by transforming unacceptable trajectories into acceptable ones based on predefined specifications, such as rule-based constraints or training datasets.
However, these approaches often lack generalizability, adaptability and tend to be overly conservative, resulting in ineffective repairs that not only fail to mitigate safety risks sufficiently but also degrade the overall driving experience.
% Existing solutions enforce ADS compliance with fixed, human-designed regulations to ensure safety. 
% However, these regulations are often overly conservative and lack generalizability, leading to ineffective repairs that not only degrade the driving experience but also fail to adequately mitigate safety risks.
% 4. Suggestion
% \cmf{Intuitively, repair solutions should be adaptively tailored to different safety-critical scenarios—for instance, more severe danger states should necessitate stronger corrective actions, while less critical situations may require milder interventions.}
% Intuitively, repair solutions should be adaptively tailored to different safety-critical scenarios—for instance, more severe danger states should necessitate stronger corrective actions, while less critical situations may require milder interventions.
% 5. Our solution -> Method

To address this issue, we propose Adaptive Decision Repair (\tool), a novel and effective repair method that identifies safety-critical states through offline learning from failed tests and generates appropriate mitigation actions to improve ADS safety.
% , as well as minimize intervention impact.
% learns from failed tests to identify safety-critical situations and generates mitigation actions to improve ADS safety, minimizing intervention impact.  
% associate them with adaptive repair actions, improving ADS safety while minimizing intervention impact.  
% Specifically, we design a transformer-based model with two joint heads, \monitor{} and \repair{}, to capture the complex dynamics and interactions within driving environments 
% while jointly linking safety severity to the repair action space. \
Specifically, \tool{} incorporates a transformer-based model with two joint heads, \monitor{} and \repair{}, designed to capture complex driving environment interactions to evaluate state safety severity and generate adaptive repair actions.
Given the absence of oracles for state safety identification, we first pretrain \tool{} using supervised learning with coarse annotations, i.e., labeling states preceding violations as positive samples and others as negative samples.
It establishes \tool{}'s foundational capability to mitigate safety-critical violations, though it may result in somewhat conservative mitigation strategies.
Therefore, we subsequently fine-tune \tool{} using reinforcement learning to improve its initial capability and generate more precise and contextually appropriate repair decisions.

% To enable \tool{} to effectively learn adaptive and optimal repair decisions with minimal intervention impact, we first pretrain it using supervised learning with coarse annotations, ensuring a foundational ability to mitigate safety-critical violations (i.e., collisions). 
% We then fine-tune \tool{} using reinforcement learning to encourage the exploration of more optimal repair decisions based on this initial capability, further enhancing its repair performance.  
% % 6. Our method -> some details
% Specifically, ReFT first warms up \tool through supervised training using coarsely defined annotations to establish a foundational repair strategy. Then, we employ online reinforcement learning, leveraging the DQN algorithm, to further fine-tune \tool. In this process, an abundance of repair decisions are automatically sampled during driving execution, with rewards naturally derived from execution outcomes. 
% This enables \tool to iteratively refine and adapt its repair decisions, improving its generalization across various driving scenarios.
% 7. Our method -> results
We evaluated \tool on two leading ADSs within the CARLA simulator, including an end-to-end ADS \textit{Roach} and a module-based ADS \textit{Pylot}.
Extensive experimental results demonstrate that \tool{} effectively mitigates safety-critical collisions, improving safety by an average of 80.5\% and outperforming the best baseline by 32.8\%. Morevoer, \tool{} achieves better repair performance by introducing fewer interventions on the original ADS decisions, with an average intervention intensity of only 0.75, outperforming the best baseline by 25.7\%. 
