\section{Discussion}
[Sketch for now - can put in paragraph form later]
\begin{itemize}
    \item We present a picture in which symmetry licenses extrapolation. By the nature of extrapolation, this amounts to an account of how large examples are determined by smaller ones. To situate our contribution, we can draw a contrast with structural recursion, the usual way of going from large to small. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For recursion, there are two degrees of freedom: the base case and ``step" function. The function can itself hide arbitrary length-dependent behavior. 
        \item For FIs, the base case (behavior on doubletons) is the only degree of freedom; \msf{amal} is the equivalent of step function, and it is fixed for all functions in the FI class. 
        \item Thus, filter invariance is a rigid enough condition to reduce all flexibility to the base case, but is flexible enough to still include a diverse set of interesting functions. In future work, we can ask whether such conditions exist. Is there any precise sense in which FIs are the ``largest" class of base-case-determined functions?
    \end{itemize}
    \item We have argued that FIs support length-independent extrapolation in a quantitative sense. But it's clear that there are other functions that don't have pathological length dependence, but which are not FI. Future work should give an account of them.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item We account for some further examples within our current framework. For example, the functions \msf{swapPairs}, which exchanges adjacent elements in a list, and \msf{swapBlocks}, which exchanges the first and second halves of a list, are not FI, but there is a sense in which they are \emph{products} of FI functions (\msf{reverse} and \msf{identity} in either order). 
        \item Ripple-carry addition is an interesting example that clearly doesn't fit our FI framework, in particular because its local behavior is not independent across subproblems: adjacent digits have to communicate via carries. Still, this communication is itself length-independent, so we hope some ``higher-order" version of our framework could accommodate such cases. 
    \end{itemize}
    \item FIs are determined by their behavior on doubletons; presumably there's a larger class of functions determined by tripletons. Is it characterized by a symmetry?
    \item Filter and map are two interesting symmetries of list functions. But still more symmetries certainly exist to be studied. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The uniquification function (\msf{nub} in Haskell) has a symmetry class different from both \msf{filter} and \msf{map}. 
        \item The \msf{double} function has interesting symmetry class that includes some FIs, like \msf{reverse}, \msf{double} itself, and \msf{filter}, but excludes others, like \msf{sort}.
        \item There is a class of functions that operates on their inputs as an undifferentiated block, including \msf{double}, \msf{identity} and \msf{reverse}. We conjecture that functions are the symmetry class of some collection of operators. 
    \end{itemize}
    Looking further, one could imagine a sort of ``Erlangen program for list functions" that would enumerate these and other symmetries, and map out how they relate to one another. 
    \item Finally, we have hinted at ways that \msf{filter} symmetry could serve as inductive bias to improve length-generalization in machine learning models. Future work could pin this down and put it into practice. 
\end{itemize}


