\subsection{Defeasible Conditionals over Compound Attributes}
\label{subsection:defeasible-conditionals-fca}
We now introduce the `$\twiddle$' as a connective over compound attributes. Then, $\phi \twiddle \psi$ is to be interpreted as ``The typical objects that satisfy the (compound) attribute $\phi$ also satisfy $\psi$''. Implicit in this reading is that a suitable structure for defining a semantics must have an explicit notion of when an object is `typical' and when it is not; i.e., a preference relation. It is then obvious that a formal context alone is insufficient. 

We introduce a \textit{preferential context} as an extension to the formal context, which provides such a relation on objects. \Cref{definition:minimisation} provides a useful notation for referring the minimal (preferred) objects from a set.  

% \\ 

% We now assume the use of compound attributes by default, and are ready to define a defeasible conditional in FCA. We begin by introducing the structure and semantics for an operator $\twiddle$ and show that it induces a preferential consequence relation. After this we make a slight modification which yields a rational consequence relation.

% The defeasible connective $\twiddle$ describes a binary relation over compound attributes, such that $\phi \twiddle \psi$ should be read as `The typical objects that satisfy the (compound) attribute $\phi$ also satisfy $\psi$'.

% Implicit in this description is the idea that a suitable semantics for defeasible conditionals requires a clear notion of exactly which objects are typical, and which are not. It is then obvious that a formal context alone is insufficient as it contains no such preference ordering. For the time being, we remain agnostic as to how one might arrive on such an order (see: \Cref{subsection:finding-order} for discussion on this matter). Then, we introduce a \textit{preferential context} as a suitable structure to provide the desired semantics.

% Before this, it is useful to introduce some notation which makes reference to the minimal objects in some subset explicit.

\begin{definition}[Minimisation]
	\label{definition:minimisation}
    Let $A$ be a set equipped with a strict partial order $\prec$. The \emph{minimisation} of ${B}$ is a selection function $\underline{B} : 2^A \mapsto 2^A$ that maps each subset $B\subseteq A$ to the set of all $\prec$-minimal elements of $B$, i.e., 
%
    \[\underline{B} = \{g \in B \mid \nexists h \in B \text{ such that } h \prec g\}\]   
\end{definition}

We can then introduce a preferential context, as well as a notion for when a defeasible conditional is satisfied

\begin{definition}[Preferential Context]
	\label{definition:preferential-context}
	A \emph{preferential context} $\mathbb{P} = (G,M,I,\prec)$ is a quadruple where  $(G,M,I)$ is a formal context and $\prec$ is a strict partial order over the set of objects $G$ representing a preference relation. 

    A \textit{defeasible conditional} $\psi \twiddle \phi$ over the set $\mathcal{L}(M)$ of compound attributes is \emph{satisfied} by a preferential context $\mathbb{P}$ if and only if for each object $g \in \minDerivation{\psi}$ it is also the case that $g \Vdash \phi$; which can be expressed equivalently as $\minDerivation{\psi} \subseteq \phi^\downarrow$, or $\phi^\downarrow \subseteq \underline{(\psi)}^{\downarrow \uparrow}$.
\end{definition}

