\subsubsection{Robustness Under Perturbed Conditions}
Under perturbed conditions with step disturbance (0.20 at $t = 15$s) and measurement noise ($\sigma = 0.20$ at $t = 20$s) as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:nonminphase_disturbance}, all controllers maintain stability whilst exhibiting distinct recovery characteristics. DRL algorithms demonstrate faster disturbance recovery than LQI, with TD3 returning to within 2\% of reference in approximately 3.50s compared to LQI's 6.00s. When noise is introduced, TD3 maintains the tightest output bounds whilst TD-MPC2 shows increased sensitivity with larger variations.

The results highlight fundamental trade-offs in non-minimum phase control: aggressive controllers (TD/-MPC2, PPO) achieve faster response at the cost of overshoot and control effort, whilst conservative approaches (TD3, DDPG, LQI) prioritise stability. TD3 emerges as the most balanced solution, combining reasonable speed, minimal overshoot, and robust disturbance rejection, critical attributes for practical implementation in chemical process control where both performance and stability are essential.

