\section{Introduction}



Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly emerging as transformative tools across a wide array of applications \citep{achiam2023gpt, guo2024large, thirunavukarasu2023large,nam2024using}. Among these, reasoning serves as a core capability underpinning tasks such as problem-solving \citep{lu2023mathvista}, scientific question answering \citep{guo2024can}, and code analysis \citep{nam2024using}. Consequently, a growing body of research has sought to evaluate and enhance the reasoning abilities of LLMs from multiple perspectives \citep{wei2022chain,guo2025deepseek,guo2024can}.
Within this broader landscape, logical reasoning stands out as a particularly challenging and intellectually demanding domain \citep{saparov2022language}. It requires a synthesis of natural language understanding, formal logical interpretation, and multi-step inferential processing \citep{patel2024multi, saparov2023testing, morishita2024enhancing}. 

