Despite growing interest in the logical reasoning capabilities of LLMs, most existing benchmarks focus narrowly on whether a model produces the correct final answer \citep{patel2024multi,parmar2024logicbench,han2022folio}. This binary evaluation, typically assessing only the correctness of a “True” or “False” output, can be misleading, as it fails to determine whether the model arrived at the answer through valid multi-step reasoning \citep{saparov2022language}. Consequently, correct answers may reflect guesswork rather than genuine logical inference. We are thus motivated  to address 
\textbf{RQ1: How to rigorously evaluate LLMs’ step-by-step correctness in logical reasoning tasks, beyond the binary evaluation of the final answer?}

In parallel with benchmarking efforts, numerous methods have been proposed to enhance the multi-step logical reasoning abilities of LLMs. While many
%Many of these approaches 
leverage inference-time strategies \citep{wang2025stepwise}, in-context learning \citep{creswell2022selection, xu2024faithful}, or external logical verifiers \citep{pan2023logic} to guide the model toward more rigorous reasoning, %. While such methods can improve performance, they primarily operate at inference time and do not directly enhance the model’s internal reasoning mechanisms.
some recent studies explored supervised fine-tuning (SFT) as a more direct approach to enhancing logical reasoning \citep{morishita2024enhancing, feng2023language}. For example, \citet{morishita2024enhancing} proposes a synthetic logic corpus designed to offer broad and systematic coverage of logical knowledge. However, it remains unclear for this important question, 
\textbf{RQ2: What style of training data, natural language or formal logical symbols, better facilitates the learning of multi-step logical reasoning through SFT?}
Addressing this research question is important for understanding how to most effectively instill logical reasoning capabilities in LLMs. %Although supervised fine-tuning is increasingly adopted to improve LLM reasoning, it remains unclear which types of supervision actually lead to the acquisition of generalizable and structured reasoning capabilities. Without this understanding, current efforts risk relying on heuristics or domain intuition.

