      \field{abstract}{Performing calculations modulo a set of relations is a basic technique in algebra. For instance, computing the inverse of an integer modulo a prime integer or computing the inverse of the complex number 3 + 2\emph{t} modulo the relation \&ell;\textsuperscript{2} + 1 = 0. Computing modulo a set \emph{S} containing more than one relation requires from \emph{S} to have some mathematical structure. For instance, computing the inverse of \emph{p} = \emph{x} + \emph{y} modulo \emph{S} = \{\vphantom\}\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} + \emph{y} + 1,\emph{y}\textsuperscript{2} + \emph{x} + 1\vphantom\{\} is difficult unless one realizes that this question is equivalent to computing the inverse of \emph{p} modulo \emph{C} = \{\vphantom\}\emph{x}\textsuperscript{4} + 2\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} + \emph{x} + 2,\emph{y} + \emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} + 1\vphantom\{\}. Indeed, from there one can simplify \emph{p} using \emph{y} = -\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} - 1 leading to \emph{q} = -\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} + \emph{x} - 1 and compute the inverse of \emph{q} modulo \emph{x}\textsuperscript{4} + 2\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} + \emph{x} + 2 (using the extended Euclidean algorithm) leading to -1/2\emph{x}\textsuperscript{3} - 1/2\emph{x}. One commonly used mathematical structure for a set of algebraic relations is that of a \emph{Gr\&ouml;bner basis.} It is particularly well suited for deciding whether a quantity is null or not modulo a set of relations. For inverse computations, the notion of a \emph{regular chain} is more adequate. For instance, computing the inverse of \emph{p} = \emph{x} + \emph{y} modulo the set \emph{C} = \{\vphantom\}\emph{y}\textsuperscript{2} - 2\emph{x} + 1,\emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} - 3\emph{x} + 2\vphantom\{\}, which is both a Gr\&ouml;bner basis and a regular chain, is easily answered in this latter point of view. Indeed, it naturally leads to consider the GCD of \emph{p} and \emph{C{$<$}inf{$>$}y{$<$}/inf{$>$}} = \emph{y}\textsuperscript{2} - 2\emph{x} + 1 modulo the relation \emph{C{$<$}inf{$>$}x{$<$}/inf{$>$}} = \emph{x}\textsuperscript{2} - 3\emph{x} + 2 = 0, which is [EQUATION] This shows that \emph{p} has no inverse if \emph{x} = 1 and has an inverse (which can be computed and which is -\emph{y} + 2) if \emph{x} = 2.}
      \field{issn}{0163-5824}
