      \field{abstract}{A roadmap for an algebraic set \$V\$ defined by polynomials with coefficients in some real field, say \${\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}\$, is an algebraic curve contained in \$V\$ whose intersection with all connected components of \$V{\textbackslash}cap{\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{n\}\$ is connected. These objects, introduced by Canny, can be used to answer connectivity queries over \$V{\textbackslash}cap {\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{n\}\$ provided that they are required to contain the finite set of query points \${\textbackslash}mathcal\{P\}{\textbackslash}subset V\$; in this case,we say that the roadmap is associated to \$(V, {\textbackslash}mathcal\{P\})\$. In this paper, we make effective a connectivity result we previously proved, to design a Monte Carlo algorithm which, on input (i) a finite sequence of polynomials defining \$V\$ (and satisfying some regularity assumptions) and (ii) an algebraic representation of finitely many query points \${\textbackslash}mathcal\{P\}\$ in \$V\$, computes a roadmap for \$(V, {\textbackslash}mathcal\{P\})\$. This algorithm generalizes the nearly optimal one introduced by the last two authors by dropping a boundedness assumption on the real trace of \$V\$. The output size and running times of our algorithm are both polynomial in \$(nD){\textasciicircum}\{n{\textbackslash}log d\}\$, where \$D\$ is the maximal degree of the input equations and \$d\$ is the dimension of \$V\$. As far as we know, the best previously known algorithm dealing with such sets has an output size and running time polynomial in \$(nD){\textasciicircum}\{n{\textbackslash}log{\textasciicircum}2 n\}\$.}
