\begin{abstract}
Picker-to-parts pallet warehouses often face inefficiencies due to conventional layouts causing excessive travel distances and high labor requirements. This study introduces a novel layout design inspired by CPU architecture, partitioning warehouse space into specialized zones— Performance (P), Efficiency (E), and Shared (S). Discrete-event simulation is used to evaluate this design against traditional rectangular (random and ABC storage) and Flying-V layouts. Results demonstrate significant improvements in throughput time and reduced labor requirements, highlighting the potential for CPU-based layouts in optimizing warehouse operations.

\keywords{Warehouse Layout \and CPU-Inspired Design \and Picker-to-Parts \and Simulation.}
\end{abstract}
%
%
%
\section{Introduction}
Order picking is critical to warehouse operations, often accounting for more than half of operational costs. Effective layout design significantly impacts picking efficiency by influencing picker travel distances, order accuracy, and operational throughput. An optimized layout minimizes unnecessary movement and improves retrieval speeds, thereby directly reducing labor costs and enhancing productivity \cite{DeKoster2007}.

Picker-to-parts systems frequently struggle with excessive picker travel, inefficient inventory allocation, and suboptimal space utilization. Traditional orthogonal warehouse layouts are prevalent due to their simplicity but are inefficient in minimizing travel distances. Non-orthogonal layouts, such as Fishbone and Flying-V, aim to reduce picker travel, albeit at the expense of increased complexity and spatial inefficiency \cite{Cardona2012},\cite{Gue2009}.

To address these issues, we introduce a CPU-inspired layout design that segments warehouse space into Performance (P), Efficiency (E), and Shared (S) zones, optimizing labor efficiency and reducing travel distances. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of this novel layout through detailed discrete-event simulation.

\section{Related Work}

A broad range of warehouse layout strategies has been studied to improve picker travel efficiency, space usage, and labor performance. Orthogonal layouts with straight, parallel aisles are common due to their simplicity, but they often lead to long travel paths. Fishbone and Flying-V layouts were introduced to mitigate this by optimizing aisle orientation. The Fishbone layout introduces angled aisles to shorten walking distances, though it typically increases overall area requirements \cite{Cardona2012}, \cite{Gue2009}. Flying-V layouts offer travel improvements through directed aisle orientation without significant structural disruption \cite{Roodbergen2011}.

Storage policy plays a crucial role in layout performance. ABC storage is one of the most popular strategies, assigning high-turnover SKUs to locations close to dispatch areas \cite{Petersen2004}. However, its effectiveness depends on proper zoning, and fixed allocations may underperform in dynamic environments \cite{Silva2022}. More adaptive approaches, such as mixed-shelves or hybrid allocation models, have been proposed to combine flexibility with accessibility \cite{Rasmi2022}.

Discrete-event simulation (DES) is widely used to evaluate these layout-policy combinations. Compared to analytical or rule-based models, DES allows for more detailed modeling of warehouse operations, including routing dynamics, congestion, and stochastic order arrivals \cite{Law2014}. Saderova et al. \cite{Saderova2022} and Derhami et al. \cite{Derhami2020} emphasize that simulation is essential to test innovative configurations that do not lend themselves to closed-form analysis.

Despite progress in layout simulation, few studies investigate physically zoned layouts inspired by architectural principles. The CPU layout proposed here draws from processor core segmentation to create modular, zone-based storage with class- and height-aware assignment. This integrated zoning model—combining Performance (P), Efficiency (E), and Shared (S) areas—is, to the best of our knowledge, not found in prior warehouse layout literature (such as in \cite{Gu2010}).

\section{CPU-Based Layout Methodology}
The CPU-based layout in Figure~\ref{fig:cpu-archi} is inspired by the structural logic of the Intel Core i5 series Processor, segmenting warehouse spaces into specialized zones:

\textbf{Performance Zone (P-Zone):} Ground-level storage nearest outbound areas for high-frequency SKUs, minimizing retrieval times.

\textbf{Efficiency Zone (E-Zone):} High-density vertical storage racks for low-frequency SKUs, maximizing spatial efficiency.

\textbf{Shared Zone (S-Zone):} Flexible buffer storage for overflow and mid-frequency SKUs.

The CPU layout evaluated in this study represents a unified design based on the performance-efficiency-sharing zoning logic, without sub-variants. It combines centralized P-Zones, high-density E-Zones, and buffer S-Zones for balanced performance and scalability.
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{cpu_layout_structure.png}
    \caption{CPU microarchitecture-based block layout.}
    \label{fig:cpu-archi}
\end{figure}

\section{Simulation Methodology}
The objective of the simulation is to evaluate the operational performance of the proposed CPU-based layout in comparison with reference layouts under realistic order picking conditions.

A comprehensive discrete-event simulation model was developed using Siemens Tecnomatix Plant Simulation, a widely used industry tool for modeling logistics systems. The simulation architecture includes multiple submodules simulating key warehouse operations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inbound Flow:} Pallets arrive following a log-normal distribution and are assigned to storage locations based on SKU classification. Each pallet is categorized by one of six collar-based height classes (1 collar = 200 mm; total pallet height from 200 mm to 1200 mm).

    \item \textbf{Order Generation:} Customer orders are released every 25 minutes, composed of pallets drawn from an 80/15/5 ABC distribution. Each order contains multiple SKUs and simulates real production demand patterns.

    \item \textbf{Reach Truck Behavior:} Inbound and outbound reach trucks are modeled as mobile agents with capacity constraints (up to 9 collar units). Routing logic follows shortest-path algorithms constrained by aisle width and traffic flow.

    \item \textbf{Storage and Retrieval Logic:} High-frequency SKUs are placed in ground-level P-Zones, while low-frequency items are directed to E-Zones. Overflow or mid-frequency items are handled via S-Zones with flexible storage assignment.

    \item \textbf{Pallet Properties:} Two pallet types are considered: Euro pallets (1200 mm × 800 mm) and Half Euro pallets (800 mm × 600 mm). Each pallet is assigned a height class between 1 and 6 collars, influencing both zone assignment and reach truck handling time.

    \item \textbf{Simulation Settings:} The system is simulated over 80 operational days, excluding warm-up periods. Performance is evaluated via average metrics across replications using common random numbers to ensure comparability.
\end{itemize}
\subsection*{Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)}

The simulation evaluates the performance of each warehouse layout based on four KPIs defined as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Throughput Time (minutes):} Time from order release in the warehouse to completion of pallet picking for outbound delivery. This reflects internal warehouse efficiency and excludes external transport delays.

    \item \textbf{Required Full-Time Equivalents (FTE):} Number of full-time employees needed to operate reach trucks and complete order picking across shifts. It is calculated as the number of active roles per shift multiplied by two.

    \item \textbf{On-Time Percentage (\%):} Share of orders completed within the allotted takt time (25 minutes). This measures the system’s punctuality and ability to support just-in-time production.

    \item \textbf{Required Warehouse Area (m\textsuperscript{2}):} Total floor space occupied by the storage layout, excluding staging or transport buffers. It is based on the modeled aisle geometry and rack placement.
\end{itemize}


The simulation model reflects a realistic warehouse environment, accounting for stochastic variability in order composition and traffic congestion, and allows for robust comparison between alternative layouts. It is based on a manual pallet warehouse handling approximately 2,000 to 2,100 pallets per day. The facility operates with reach trucks in narrow aisles and employs double-deep racking across a footprint of about 4,000 to 4,500 m\textsuperscript{2}. Inbound pallets are received continuously, while outbound orders are processed in timed batches. Constraints such as aisle width, vehicle turning space, and stacking height were incorporated to ensure realism and operational relevance. The current warehouse layout is visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:current-layout}. The warehouse is filled with pallets, the outbound reach trucks (in blue) retrieve these pallets from the warehouse. The inbound reach trucks (in orange) bring the pallets into the warehouse.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{current_layout.png}
    \caption{Visual representation of the current layout.}
    \label{fig:current-layout}
\end{figure}

\section{Results}
\subsection{Visualized Warehouse Layouts for Comparison}
We show in this subsection the visual representation of different layouts generated in the simulation. The orthogonal layout is characterized by parallel aisles and perpendicular cross aisles (see Figure~\ref{fig:c-layout}). 
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering  \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{c1_random.png}
    \caption{Visual representation of conventional layout.}
    \label{fig:c-layout}
\end{figure}

The Flying-V layout aligns aisles diagonally towards outbound areas, optimizing picker travel paths (see Figure~\ref{fig:fish_v}). Although this layout achieves reduced travel distances, it typically requires more area due to its non-linear aisle configuration and can introduce complexity in warehouse operations.
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{fish_v.png}
    \caption{Visual representation of Flying-V layout.}
    \label{fig:fish_v}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{CPU-based-layout.png}
    \caption{Visual representation of CPU-based layout.}
    \label{fig:cpu-layout}
\end{figure}
\subsection{Comparison of Storage Policies and Layouts}

The layouts evaluated in this study differ not only in physical structure but also in storage policies. The CPU-based layout employs a structured, zone-specific class-based storage policy (see Figure~\ref{fig:cpu-layout}). A-class SKUs (high-frequency) are assigned to P-Zones (in color blue) for fast access and minimal picker travel. C-class SKUs (low-frequency) are stored in high-density E-Zones (in color orange) to maximize space utilization. B-class and overflow SKUs are allocated to flexible S-Zones (in color yellow) to balance load and adapt to demand fluctuations. Pallet height is also considered—taller pallets are excluded from ground-level P-Zones and are preferentially routed to E-Zones or upper levels of S-Zones.

In contrast, the \textbf{Conv.–Random} layout allocates SKUs without consideration of demand frequency, resulting in inefficient retrieval times—especially for fast-moving items. The \textbf{Conv.–Class} layout applies a class-based policy but lacks the dedicated zoning of the CPU design, instead concentrating A-class items near outbound areas within a flat structure. \textbf{Flying-V (ABC)} maintains the same ABC storage logic but improves picker routing through aisle reorientation towards the outbound zone.

The simulation results are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:results}. The comparison focuses on four representative configurations: \textbf{Conv.–Random}, \textbf{Conv.–Class}, \textbf{Flying-V (Random)}, \textbf{Flying-V (ABC)}, and the proposed \textbf{CPU} layout. These were selected to represent a variety of storage policies and spatial designs.


\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Performance Comparison of Layouts — throughput time in minutes, labor in FTEs, and area in square meters}

\label{tab:results}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
Layout & Throughput Time (min:sec) & FTE & On-Time (\%) & Area (m$^2$) \\
\hline
Current & 16.25 & 26 & 98.82 & 7249\\
Conv.–Random & 16:08 & 20 & 100 & 4023 \\
Conv.–Class & 15:53 & 20 & 100 & 4023 \\
Flying-V (Random) & 16:03 & 20 & 100 & 4477 \\
Flying-V (ABC) & 15:49 & 20 & 100 & 4477 \\
CPU & 14:56 & 20 & 100 & 4112\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


All tested layouts deliver performance improvements across key KPIs compared to the existing warehouse configuration. The use of ABC classification in the Conv.–Class layout significantly reduces picker travel time relative to Conv.–Random. The Flying-V layout offers further gains by orienting aisles toward the outbound zone, improving routing efficiency. Among the alternatives, the CPU layout achieves the shortest throughput time and maintains high on-time performance while using slightly more space.

Although the CPU layout requires a modest increase in floor area (+2.2\% compared to the conventional layout), this trade-off is justified by its significant improvement in throughput efficiency. Throughput time—defined as the interval from order release to pallet consolidation—is a critical performance driver in high-volume warehouses. Even small reductions at the order level can translate into substantial time and labor savings. The CPU layout shortens average throughput time by approximately 1–2 minutes compared to other configurations, resulting in several hours of operational gain per day. It also lowers buffer requirements, reduces cycle time variability, and minimizes late-order risk. Thus, despite its slightly larger footprint, the CPU layout offers strong advantages in temporal efficiency and process stability.



\section{Discussion}
The results illustrate clear advantages of CPU-based layouts. The CPU layout achieves superior picker efficiency through optimal P-Zone placement, despite a slightly larger area requirement. It provides a balanced approach, improving space efficiency and throughput, suitable for environments with spatial constraints. This research is the first attempt to see the performance of this layout. How to find out the best size of different zones is not discussed here. But we make the a slit different layout illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:cpu-layout-2} to store the same number of pallets but with larger P-zones. We can get already even better throughput time, namely 14:40 minutes. 
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{cpu-layout-2.png}
    \caption{Visualization of the changed areas of CPU-based layout.}
    \label{fig:cpu-layout-2}
\end{figure}
%\subsection*{Model Validation and Limitations}

%The simulation logic was validated by benchmarking throughput times and resource utilization against historical data from a real-world warehouse with similar structure and volume (approx. 2,000 pallets/day). Expert feedback from warehouse supervisors was used to refine route logic and pallet handling rules.

Limitations include simplifications in picker behavior and congestion. The model assumes ideal travel speeds and no delays due to real-time obstruction or equipment failure. Pallet load diversity and replenishment strategies are also abstracted. Despite this, the model captures relative layout performance reliably under stable order conditions.

\section{Conclusion}
The CPU-inspired layout demonstrates significant potential for improving efficiency in manual picker-to-parts warehouses. The strategic division into specialized zones results in improved throughput times, reduced labor requirements, and optimized space utilization. Future studies should explore integration with zoning and dynamic SKU allocation to further optimize performance.

